  "image_name": "2d75c0520b7828b1f4924b117dd8512e.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares two lithium electrolyte environments and the interphases they form on a Li4Ti5O12 electrode. On the left, a very concentrated water-in-salt electrolyte shows lithium ions mainly surrounded by water and some TFSI-related species in the primary solvation sheath. This configuration is associated with stronger water reduction at the electrode and produces a mostly inorganic, LiF-rich solid electrolyte interphase (SEI).\n\nOn the right, the electrolyte contains LiTFSI, KOH, urea, and water at lower concentration. Here, urea molecules visibly enter the lithium solvation sheath alongside water and TFSI. The figure indicates that this modified solvation structure suppresses or limits water reduction compared with the water-in-salt case. As a result, the interphase on Li4Ti5O12 becomes more complex: an inorganic LiF layer remains, but it is covered by an additional organic, urea-derived layer labeled as polyurea.\n\nOverall, the visual message is that adding urea changes the local coordination around Li+, reduces parasitic water reduction, and converts a purely LiF-rich SEI into a hybrid interphase containing both inorganic LiF and an organic polyurea component.",
